---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Cookie Policy"
  description="Cookie policy for Behavioral Psychology: what cookies are, how this site uses them, your privacy choices, and how to manage them."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <span>Cookie Policy</span></p>
      <h1>Cookie Policy</h1>
      <p>What cookies are, how this site uses them, and how you can control them. Last updated: November 2026.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What Cookies Are</h2>
        <p>A cookie is a small text file that a website asks your browser to store on your device. When you return to the site, your browser sends the cookie back. Sites use cookies for things like remembering preferences, keeping you logged in, measuring traffic, and serving relevant ads. Cookies are not programs, and they cannot run code on your device.</p>

        <h2>How This Site Uses Cookies</h2>
        <p>Behavioral Psychology is a static informational website. There are no user accounts, no comment system, and no login, so the site has no functional need for cookies of its own. At launch:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The site sets no first-party cookies for tracking or analytics.</li>
          <li>The site runs no analytics scripts, so your visits are not recorded in an analytics dashboard.</li>
          <li>The site embeds no third-party widgets, video players, or social feeds that would set their own cookies.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In short, reading this site does not place tracking cookies on your device.</p>

        <h2>Advertising Cookies in the Future</h2>
        <p>This site intends to apply for Google AdSense to help support its publishing costs. Until that approval happens, no advertising cookies are set. If the site is approved, the ad network may set cookies to measure ad performance and, where applicable, personalize advertising. When that change happens, this policy will be updated to describe exactly which cookies are in use and what they do, and the <a href="/privacy-policy/">privacy policy</a> will be updated at the same time.</p>

        <h2>Managing Cookies in Your Browser</h2>
        <p>You can control cookies through your browser settings at any time, regardless of what this or any other site does:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>View and delete cookies:</strong> every major browser lets you see which cookies are stored and delete some or all of them.</li>
          <li><strong>Block cookies:</strong> you can block all cookies, or block only third-party cookies, in your browser's privacy or security settings.</li>
          <li><strong>Private browsing:</strong> private or incognito windows discard most cookies when the window is closed.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Blocking all cookies will not break this site, because the site does not depend on cookies to function.</p>

        <h2>Do Not Track</h2>
        <p>Some browsers send a "Do Not Track" signal. Because this site performs no tracking of its own, there is nothing to switch off in response to that signal. If advertising is added in the future, ad-network behavior in response to the signal will be governed by the ad network's own policies.</p>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>Questions about this policy can be sent to <a href="mailto:info@behavioralpsychology.pro">info@behavioralpsychology.pro</a> or through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
